"Fills the space between a time series and a meaningful baseline to emphasize quantitative magnitude over time.\n\nUse when: One nonnegative series needs a stronger sense of accumulated extent. A zero reference has a defensible interpretation for the measured quantity.\n\nAvoid when: Several overlapping fills would hide each other or values oscillate around an arbitrary reference.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes time. Height and filled extent above zero encode the series magnitude.\n\nBuild: Sort and aggregate timestamps at a stated temporal resolution. Draw a zero-based value scale and fill the area beneath the observed profile. Show missing intervals as gaps and label units and reporting period.\n\nCheck: Check the baseline is zero unless a different reference is explicitly meaningful. Verify time intervals have proportional widths. Confirm any interpolation matches the sampling process and avoids invented peaks.\n\nPitfalls: Area can suggest a total even when integrating the quantity has no sensible meaning. Overlaid areas conceal values; use separate panels or lines for several series.\n\nExample: Show synthetic hourly electricity demand across a day with a zero baseline and an explicitly labeled kW axis.\n\nAlternatives: Line chart, Stacked area chart, Difference chart."
